\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Martingale.eq_zero_of_predictable_finiteVariation_bounded_continuous}
  \uses{def:Martingale, def:LocallyBoundedVariationOn, lem:BoundedVariationOn.sq_increment_sum_tendsto_zero_of_uniform_bound}
  \lean{MeasureTheory.Martingale.eq_zero_of_predictable_finiteVariation_bounded_continuous}
Let \(M\) be a strongly predictable càdlàg true martingale with \(M_0=0\),
and suppose every path has locally bounded variation.  For every
deterministic time \(t>0\), the bounded-continuous finite-variation bridge
shows that \(M_t=0\) almost surely.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Stop the process on a deterministic horizon and localize further so that the
stopped martingale is bounded.  After the predictable-jump part has been
removed, work with the continuous finite-variation process on that horizon.
Choose deterministic partitions whose mesh tends to zero.  Martingale
increments over disjoint partition intervals are orthogonal, so
\[
  \mathbb E[M_t^2]
  =
  \sum_{k<n}\mathbb E[(M_{s_{k+1}}-M_{s_k})^2].
\]
For each sample path, continuity makes the largest increment along these
partitions tend to \(0\), while finite variation gives a finite variation
bound.  Hence
Lemma~\ref{lem:BoundedVariationOn.sq_increment_sum_tendsto_zero_of_uniform_bound}
shows that the pathwise square-increment sums tend to \(0\).  Boundedness of
the stopped martingale gives a dominating integrable random variable, so
dominated convergence sends the expectations of the square-increment sums to
\(0\).  Thus \(\mathbb E[M_t^2]=0\), and therefore \(M_t=0\) almost surely.
\end{proof}
